# Facet — Screens, menus, and audio

This file defines the screens Facet opens on and plays on, how a player moves
between them, and audio. The rules those screens present are in
`specs/rules.md`, the board's geometry and its gems are in `specs/board.md`, and
the keyboard and pointer input the screens read are in `specs/controls.md`.
Every figure and every piece of screen copy below carries the name this
specification gives it.

## Presentation

Facet fixes no palette, no font, no gem artwork, no effect, and no background.
The look of the board and the screens around it is yours. `specs/overview.md`
states the visual design, `specs/board.md` states the few things about a gem a
player has to read at a glance, and `specs/assets.md` states the produced files
that carry them. One requirement is this file's own: every piece of text a
screen shows is legible against whatever sits behind it at the logical stage
size `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`).

## Screens

`state.screen` names the screen the game is in, and it is one of `title`,
`howto`, `playing`, `paused`, and `gameover`.

### `title`

The main menu, and where the game opens.

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Title | `TITLE_TEXT` | `FACET` |
| Tagline | `TAGLINE_TEXT` | `PRESSURE FINDS THE FLAW` |
| Menu | `TITLE_ITEMS` | `PLAY`, `HOW TO PLAY`, in that order |

The menu's items are stacked one above the next under the title and tagline. The
item at `state.menuIndex` is highlighted and drawn distinctly from the others,
so a player always sees which item `confirm` would accept. `menuIndex` is `0` on
arriving at the title. `up` and `down` move the highlight by one item and wrap
at both ends. `back` does nothing.

`confirm` takes the highlighted item:

| Item | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `PLAY` | Starts a fresh round, as `specs/rules.md` describes, and sets `screen = playing`. |
| `HOW TO PLAY` | Sets `screen = howto`. |

A round started from here is always fresh, whatever was played before it, so the
score, the level, and the board a player is handed carry nothing over from an
earlier round. No board is in play on this screen, so the title screen shows the
title, the tagline, and the menu over whatever field the look puts behind them.

### `howto`

How to play, written in a player's words rather than as rules of a system. It
covers:

- that the board is a grid of gems of seven kinds, and that a swap exchanges a
  gem with the one beside it;
- that three or more of one kind in a line shatter, and that a swap which
  shatters nothing is refused and leaves the board as it was;
- that every gem beside a shattering line takes strain, that enough strain
  leaves a gem flawed, and that a flawed gem shatters along with any clear it
  touches and is worth double;
- that a line of four leaves a brilliant, a line of five or more leaves a prism,
  and a line crossing another leaves a star;
- that a brilliant takes the ring of gems around it, a star takes its whole row
  and column, and a prism swapped with a gem takes every gem of that kind;
- that what falls into the gap can shatter again, and that each step of a chain
  is worth more than the one before it;
- that reaching the level's target opens the next level, and that a round ends
  when no swap is left that would shatter anything;
- the controls, naming the keys bound to the `pause`, `mute`, and `back`
  actions, and that a gem is played with the pointer as well as the keys.

`back` returns to `title` with `menuIndex = 0`.

### `playing`

The live board, and the only screen a swap is made on. It shows:

| Element | What the player sees |
| --- | --- |
| The board | Every cell of the board, each gem drawn at its cell center with its kind, its cut, and its strain readable. |
| The cursor | The cell at `state.cursor`, marked. |
| The selection | The cell at `state.selection` when there is one, marked distinctly from the cursor. |
| A refusal | The two cells of `state.refusal` while it stands, marked as a swap that did not take. |

The readouts sit clear of the board's extent, which `specs/board.md` gives:

| Readout | Constant | What it shows |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Score | `HUD_SCORE_LABEL` (`SCORE`) | `state.score`. |
| Level | `HUD_LEVEL_LABEL` (`LEVEL`) | `state.level`. |
| Level meter | | `state.levelScore` against the level's target, as a meter a player reads without arithmetic. |
| Chain | `HUD_CHAIN_LABEL` (`CHAIN`) | `state.multiplier`, shown while `state.phase` is `resolving` and absent while it is `idle`. |

`specs/rules.md` gives the level's target and the multiplier.

### `paused`

Play held where it stands, reached from `playing` with the `pause` action.

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Title | `PAUSED_TITLE_TEXT` | `PAUSED` |
| Menu | `PAUSED_ITEMS` | `RESUME`, `QUIT`, in that order |

`menuIndex` is `0` on arriving. `up` and `down` move the highlight by one item
and wrap at both ends. `pause` and `back` both return to `playing` with the
board exactly as it was left.

| Item | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `RESUME` | Sets `screen = playing`, with the board exactly as it was left. |
| `QUIT` | Sets `screen = title` and `menuIndex = 0`, abandoning the round. |

The board shows behind the menu, quieted, so a player reads the position while
paused.

### `gameover`

The end of a round, reached when the board settles with no legal swap on it.

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Title | `GAMEOVER_TITLE_TEXT` | `NO MOVES LEFT` |
| Menu | `GAMEOVER_ITEMS` | `PLAY AGAIN`, `QUIT`, in that order |

The screen also shows the round's final `state.score` and the `state.level` it
reached. `menuIndex` is `0` on arriving. `up` and `down` move the highlight by
one item and wrap at both ends. `back` sets `screen = title` and
`menuIndex = 0`.

| Item | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `PLAY AGAIN` | Starts a fresh round, as `specs/rules.md` describes, and sets `screen = playing`. |
| `QUIT` | Sets `screen = title` and `menuIndex = 0`. |

The final board shows behind the menu, quieted.

## What advances on each screen

`state.simTime` accumulates the frame's delta time on every update, whatever the
screen, and input is read on every screen. Beyond that:

| Screen | What advances |
| --- | --- |
| `title`, `howto`, `paused`, `gameover` | Nothing. |
| `playing` | The board and the chain resolving over it, the chain step timer, and the refusal mark. |

Every duration in this specification is counted against the delta time the
runtime hands each update, so the chain step timer and the refusal mark advance
only while `screen` is `playing`.

## Menu navigation

Menus are keyboard only, through the registered actions in `specs/controls.md`:
`up` and `down` move the highlight, `confirm` accepts the highlighted item, and
`back` returns. `menuIndex` is `0` on entering every screen, and on a screen
with no menu it stays `0`. The pointer plays the board and does not operate
menus. `mute` is read on every screen.

## Audio

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The audio layer is part of the runtime you write, and what it plays are the
produced files `specs/assets.md` lists. Define and play the eight cues in
`CUES`, under exactly these names, one per event:
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Audio plays through the engine's cue bus, each cue bound to the produced file
`specs/assets.md` lists. Define and play the eight cues in `CUES`, under exactly
these names, one per event:
{{else}}
Audio plays through the engine's cue bus: define the eight cues in `CUES` from
the game instance's `initialize`, each bound to the produced file
`specs/assets.md` lists, and play them from the world's audio inside a tick,
under exactly these names, one per event:
{{/if}}

| Cue | Constant | Played when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `select` | `CUES.select` | A cell is selected. |
| `swap` | `CUES.swap` | A swap is accepted. |
| `refuse` | `CUES.refuse` | A swap is refused. |
| `clear` | `CUES.clear` | A chain step clears its set. |
| `flaw` | `CUES.flaw` | A gem reaches `MAX_STRAIN`. |
| `cut` | `CUES.cut` | A cut gem is created. |
| `levelup` | `CUES.levelUp` | A level is completed. |
| `gameover` | `CUES.gameOver` | The round ends. |

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Each is a distinct short sound, so the eight events are told apart by ear. Each
is played on the frame its event happens, from `update`, and at most once on
that frame; a frame that raises more than one of them plays each of those once,
and a frame on which four gems reach `MAX_STRAIN` plays `flaw` once.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Each is a distinct short sound, so the eight events are told apart by ear. Each
is played on the frame its event happens, from `update`, and at most once on
that frame; a frame that raises more than one of them plays each of those once,
and a frame on which four gems reach `MAX_STRAIN` plays `flaw` once.
{{else}}
Each is a distinct short sound, so the eight events are told apart by ear. Each
is played on the frame its event happens, by the code that raised it, and at
most once on that frame; a frame that raises more than one of them plays each of
those once, and a frame on which four gems reach `MAX_STRAIN` plays `flaw` once.
{{/if}}

`clear` sounds the chain ladder, so the tone it plays is the rung
`specs/assets.md` gives for the step's multiplier. The rungs are that one cue's
sources rather than events of their own.

A cue is played by a frame, never by a pose of the debug surface.

Two produced pieces of music play under the game, and `specs/assets.md` states
what they are. The title theme loops on `title` and `howto`, and the play bed
loops on `playing`, `paused`, and `gameover`. One of the two is playing on every
screen, and the runtime's mute bit silences them along with the cues.

Muting and the first-interaction unlock belong to the runtime. The game binds
the `mute` action to the runtime's mute bit and toggles it from any screen, then
mirrors that bit into `state.muted` every frame. The game stays fully playable
with sound muted.

## Out of scope

- Network or online play.
- Touch and gamepad input. The board is played with the pointer and the
  keyboard, and the menus are driven from the keyboard.
- Persistence of progress or settings between sessions. Each session starts
  fresh.
